# Amazon Image Studio Project Rules

## Project Overview

This project is an Amazon Listing image generation tool for ecommerce SKU publishing.

Core workflow:

1. Upload product reference images.
2. Paste product Listing information, selling points, marketplace, and optional notes.
3. Choose which image tasks to generate and how many of each are needed.
4. Run AI analysis to produce Chinese-readable image strategies for the selected tasks.
5. Keep Chinese strategy text as the operator-facing source of truth.
6. Generate English execution prompts only on demand for preview, or automatically at final image-generation time.
7. Generate images through the backend image generation proxy.
8. Display, retry, continue, preview, and download generated images.

This is not a single-image generator. It is an Amazon Listing image production workflow driven by configurable image tasks.

## Tech Stack

- Frontend: React 18 + Vite, plain JavaScript JSX, component-scoped CSS files.
- Backend: Node.js 20+, Express, ES modules.
- Upload handling: multer on backend, browser-side image compression on frontend.
- AI analysis: backend route analyzes product images + Listing information + selected image tasks.
- Image generation: backend route proxies image generation API and saves results under backend/uploads.
- Dev setup: frontend runs on port 5173, backend runs on port 3001. Vite proxies /api and /uploads to backend.
- Production setup: backend can serve frontend/dist when NODE_ENV=production.

Important folders:

- frontend/src/App.jsx: main frontend workflow and task state.
- frontend/src/components: UI components.
- frontend/src/utils/requestPayload.js: request payload shaping. Keep requests compact.
- frontend/src/utils/imageTasks.js: image-task catalog and task-to-plan expansion.
- backend/server.js: Express app entry.
- backend/routes: API routes for upload, analyze, prompt preview, generate, and API-key testing.
- backend/strategy-library.js: backend hidden strategy source used by AI analysis and generation flavoring.
- backend/uploads: runtime generated/uploaded files.

## Coding Style

- Use ES modules: import/export syntax only.
- Keep React components as function components unless an Error Boundary class is required.
- Use clear camelCase for variables and functions.
- Use PascalCase for React components.
- Use kebab-case or existing naming style for CSS class names.
- Keep one component's styles in the matching .css file when possible.
- Keep comments short and useful. Do not add comments that merely restate the code.
- Prefer structured objects and arrays for tasks, request payloads, and image plans.
- Avoid ad hoc string parsing when there is already a helper in frontend/src/utils.
- Do not add placeholder or fake UI. If a control is visible, it must perform the real promised action. For example, only show "upload file" after real file upload is implemented; if only image upload is implemented, the UI must say image upload only.

## Runtime Shape Requirements

The project currently uses JavaScript, not TypeScript. Be strict with runtime shapes.

Listing state should remain compatible with:

- listingInfo
- productName
- sellingPoints
- additionalInfo
- marketplace
- imageLanguage
- fontPreference
- brandColorMode
- brandColor
- designNotes
- complexity
- selectedImageTasks
- imagePlans

Each image plan should remain compatible with:

- id: number in the current selected-task order
- name: display name for the image task
- type: image-task type or backend-derived type
- taskType: main / feature / scenario / detail / dimensions / steps / comparison / package / summary
- taskKey: stable task instance key such as feature-1
- purpose: short purpose text
- prompt: Chinese-readable operator strategy text
- promptEn: English strategy prompt preview
- executionPromptEn: final English execution prompt preview
- promptDirty: true only when the Chinese prompt was manually edited and English preview needs regeneration

Generated image results should preserve:

- imageId
- name
- taskType
- imageUrl
- status
- prompt
- promptEn
- actualResolution
- requestedResolution
- sizeMatchesRequest

Do not send empty legacy fields in requests. Use frontend/src/utils/requestPayload.js helpers.

## Image Task Rules

Current configurable task families:

- main
- feature
- scenario
- detail
- dimensions
- steps
- comparison
- package
- summary

The UI must allow users to decide which task types to generate and how many of each they need.

- Do not force a fixed 7-image output.
- Do not reintroduce required template-family selection as the main workflow.
- The backend may still use hidden strategy flavoring, but the user-facing source of truth is the selected image task list plus Chinese strategy text.

## Strategy Rules

The project still contains hidden backend strategy families used as internal visual guidance:

- basic
- featureFocus
- infographic
- lifestyle
- technical
- premium
- fashion

Rules:

- Do not re-add home as a visual strategy. Home is a product category, not a visual style.
- Do not hard-code fixed colors into prompts.
- Color should adapt to product image, material, usage scenario, and marketplace unless the user manually chooses a brand color.
- Text language on images must follow the selected image language or marketplace default.
- Keep strategy flavoring behind the scenes. Do not let it override explicit user task choices.

## Frontend Rules

- App.jsx owns high-level workflow state. Avoid moving cross-step orchestration into small UI components.
- ProductImageUploader should only compress uploads. It must not crop, pad, add white background, or visually alter product content.
- Upload compression should preserve product full view and aspect ratio.
- AmazonListingForm should treat Chinese strategy text as the primary editable content.
- English execution prompts must not auto-generate for every plan. They should be generated only on explicit preview request or at final generation time.
- AgentAnalyzer success state should stay understandable and not hide critical planning feedback too quickly.
- Keep ErrorBoundary wrapping App in main.jsx.
- Avoid exposing large internal JSON/debug data in the UI or console.
- Avoid nested cards and crowded UI for operational screens. This app is a work tool, not a marketing landing page.
- Do not allow image-task changes to silently desync imagePlans; keep plan regeneration deterministic.

## Backend Rules

- server.js should remain a thin Express entry and route mount file.
- API routes should return clear JSON with success/status/message where possible.
- Upload route should validate file type and keep uploaded URLs compatible with /uploads.
- Generate route must keep image generation behind backend. Never call image generation APIs directly from the frontend.
- Prompt-preview route exists for manual English execution-prompt preview; keep it lightweight and on-demand.
- Generate route should support reference image content types correctly: jpeg, png, webp.
- Image dimension detection should support common returned formats where possible.
- Timeout values should be configurable through environment variables.
- Do not log secrets or API keys.
- Do not return massive internal strategy-analysis objects to the frontend unless explicitly needed.

## Request Payload Rules

- Always use frontend/src/utils/requestPayload.js when shaping analyze/generate payloads.
- Keep payloads compact.
- Do not send empty strings, empty arrays, legacy fields, or duplicate fields unless required.
- AI analysis requests should include:
  - product/listing content
  - marketplace
  - additionalInfo when present
  - complexity
  - selectedImageTasks
  - referenceImages
- Generate requests should include:
  - compact listing
  - exactly one imagePlan per generation call
  - imageType when backend hidden strategy flavoring is needed
  - complexity
  - resolution
  - referenceImages

## Image Generation Rules

- User requests 2K/4K should be sent to backend as requested resolution.
- The app can request and verify dimensions, but true native 2K detail depends on the upstream image model.
- Frontend download resizing is only a fallback/export convenience; it is not native model detail.
- If generated dimensions do not match requested dimensions, preserve that information in UI.
- Do not silently claim a model generated true 2K if the actual returned file is smaller.

## Reference Image Rules

- Reference images are a product-consistency input, not just a decoration.
- The generation flow must preserve product identity across output images: shape, silhouette, structure, proportions, color, material, surface details, and accessories.
- Do not add white padding, crop away the product, or alter the product body during upload preprocessing.
- If reference-image count is intentionally limited in backend calls, keep that limitation explicit in code and UI expectations.

## Operational Pitfalls to Avoid

- Do not start local services or run git operations unless the user explicitly asks or confirms.
- Do not overwrite user changes.
- Do not reset or clean the git working tree without explicit permission.
- Do not edit backend/.env values casually.
- Do not remove runtime uploads without explicit user approval.
- Do not reintroduce hidden form sections or CSS-only-hidden legacy fields.
- Do not duplicate task or payload logic across frontend and backend without a plan to keep them synchronized.

## Verification Guidelines

For code changes, prefer:

- backend syntax checks with node --check for changed backend files.
- frontend production build with Vite when JSX/CSS changes are made.
- targeted search with rg to confirm removed legacy workflow fields are not still referenced.

Do not start dev servers automatically. Ask first if runtime browser testing is needed.
